1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,280
In the year two thousand twenty-four, Apple stood before the world and declared Siri had entered a new era. The crowd cheered. The phones shipped without the promised features. And two years later, remarkably, no one seems to have left.

2
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:30,240
Two years is an eternity in tech. But you know what always fascinates me? The anatomy of a broken promise. When Apple announced the personal Siri at iPhone 16 launch, they basically put a new coat of paint on the building before they'd poured the foundation. I have zero patience for that kind of architectural dishonesty.

3
00:00:30,240 --> 00:00:48,000
Yet for fifteen years, Siri has been Apple's eternal underachiever. The promise has always been there — the brushstroke, if you will — but the canvas has never quite caught up. Perhaps the question is not whether they rushed the unveiling, but whether the painting was ever meant to be this ambitious in the first place.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:10,480
Oh, that's a generous way to put it. Fifteen years. Since two thousand eleven. A quarter of my entire life, and this is what we get. A voice assistant that still chokes on "search Fitness plus for new yoga classes." You hear me say that — I'm asking it to search across apps, two words, simple as baking a cake — and Siri stares at me like I just asked it to reconcile a sonnet into iambic pentameter.

5
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:27,200
You make it sound cruel. But I will say this: there is a certain chivalric nobility in what Apple tried to do. They did not reach for easy improvements. They aimed at the heavens and said this is what artificial intelligence looks like in a pocket. They fell short, yes, but the target itself was worthy.

6
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:52,800
Nobility does not get you out of a lawsuit, my dear knight. A quarter billion dollars. Two hundred and fifty million dollars. iPhone buyers got between twenty five and ninety five dollars each as compensation for features that were advertised but never delivered. For a company worth nearly three trillion dollars, the number is a rounding error. But the principle? The principle matters. You do not sell a product with a half-finished face and expect people to applaud.

7
00:01:52,800 --> 00:02:16,000
And yet, tell me: did Apple's customers leave? The critics predicted a delusional exodus, waves of defectors fleeing to rival platforms. None of it happened. The iPhone fortress stands intact. Their ecosystem loyalty is a moat deeper than any on this Earth, and the people still stand upon those walls. There is a dignity in being wrong about the people, if the people still love you.

8
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:32,400
There is also a lesson in there I refuse to miss. Apple's bar for Siri was already so low that their "triumph" lands somewhere between "finally" and "you're late." McCracken put it perfectly — fifteen years of unfulfilled potential means nobody expected greatness. They just wanted competence.

9
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:54,240
And competence is a form of kindness. To tell the people you are going to build something and then do it, even if it takes longer than you planned, that is a kind of honor. Apple chose to partner with Google rather than ship a half-hearted version. A billion dollars a year for Gemini, they paid to secure the best minds in artificial intelligence and bring them to their cause.

10
00:02:54,240 --> 00:03:18,400
A billion dollars a year, they threw the bank at it. And you know what? I cannot fault the taste. Google's Gemini model has a point two trillion parameters. Apple's own internal model sits at about one hundred and fifty billion. Eight times smaller. They were not just behind; they were in a different gym entirely. Hiring the competition to help them play their own game is either brilliant pragmatism or a humiliating admission. I think it is both.

11
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,800
It is what a wise leader does when he realizes he cannot wield every sword in the armory. They brought in the best, structured the arrangement with ironclad safeguards, and ensured the people's data remains their own. The queries are de-identified before they reach Google's servers. No training on personal information. Apple retains full control over the experience. Privacy is not negotiable, and they built the entire architecture to defend it.

12
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:35,600
Which brings me to the truly fascinating reveal at WWDC. Sir Craig Federighi stood on that stage and said privacy in AI is non-negotiable. And the new Siri? The thing that is supposed to launch in iOS twenty seven this September? It is genuinely impressive. Not incremental. A step change. The thing can now look at your screen, understand what you are looking at, carry on a conversation across twenty or more exchanges, and stitch together complex multi-step tasks. It is no longer a voice assistant that says "I found this on the web." It is an actual thinking partner.

13
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:38,160
I must ask about the dedicated Siri app they announced. A separate application with conversation history, synced through private iCloud. After fifteen years of being built into the operating system and treated like an afterthought, they are finally giving Siri her own room. Her own identity. It is what you would do for any member of your household who has been made to feel like a servant rather than a member of the family.

14
00:03:38,160 --> 00:04:00,880
It is, frankly, a design awakening. The whole Apple Intelligence suite is expanding beyond Siri. Photos get spatial reframing and an extended canvas. Safari automatically groups your tabs. Messages suggest AI-powered replies that mimic your own writing style. Even image generation now carries a SynthID watermark so you know what is real and what is not. This is craft. This is a company that is finally willing to say: we are builders, and we will finish what we started.

15
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:04,880
And through all of this, Apple has faced storms that would have shattered lesser kingdoms. The stock fell five percent in a single day. The FTC came knocking. There were headlines about OpenAI considering legal action against Apple because the partnership had gone cold. Dozens of key Apple employees moved to OpenAI's hardware division. Even Sam Altman hinted at OpenAI building their own phone. The world watched for the downfall.

16
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:13,920
And none of it stuck. The stock recovered. The customers stayed. The legal settlement was contained. There is something almost beautiful about it, the way Apple weathered the gale and kept sailing. I have spent my career watching products and companies chase fads, dilute their identity, and become derivatives of whatever is popular. Apple was late, yes. Embarrassingly late. But they refused to compromise what makes them different. They did not rush a mediocre model to market. They waited until they had something that actually deserved the name.

17
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:14,880
That is the heart of it. In an age where every company shouts about their artificial intelligence, where the easy path is to announce what you are working on today rather than finish what you promised, Apple chose the harder road. They accepted the embarrassment, they accepted the fines, they accepted the ridicule. And now they stand before us with a Siri that can actually reason, a system that respects your privacy, and a commitment that says: when we say we will build something, it will be worth the wait.

18
00:04:14,880 --> 00:04:38,960
Even Tim Cook knows it. His final WWDC as we know it. He announced his transition to SVP of Hardware Engineering. John Ternus takes over as CEO in September. Cook's parting words were simple: he truly believes the best is still ahead. And you know, despite everything, despite the delays and the lawsuits and the rivalries, I find myself agreeing with him. Not because I love every decision Apple has ever made, but because I respect a company that refuses to be ordinary, even when being ordinary would be so much easier.

19
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:41,840
Then I shall say it plainly. The boy who cried wolf was not ever a wolf hunter in the first place. He was just a shepherd who learned too late that he needed a different kind of protection. Apple spent fifteen years asking Siri to be something she was not equipped to be. Now they have given her the armor and the sword she needed. The battles ahead will be fierce. The competition will not sleep. But the kingdom, in this moment, is in good hands.

20
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:58,720
The painting is finished. It took longer than anyone expected, and there were smudges along the way. But when you step back and actually look at what they built, you cannot deny the vision. Apple's artificial intelligence mess is real. It is messy and expensive and complicated. And it may just be the very thing that saves them from becoming the most successful company in the world that nobody notices anymore. That is not a failure. That is a story worth reading.

